{"conversation_id": "conv_024", "response": "There are three main types. Supervised learning is what I just described with the cats - you give the system labeled examples and it learns to map inputs to outputs. This works for things like email spam detection (labeled spam/not spam) or predicting house prices (examples with known prices).

Unsupervised learning gets data without labels and tries to find hidden structure. Like giving it customer purchase data and having it discover that customers naturally cluster into groups with similar buying patterns, without you telling it what groups to look for.

Reinforcement learning is about learning through trial and error. The system tries different actions in an environment and gets rewards or penalties, gradually learning which strategies work best. This is how systems learn to play games like chess or Go, or how robots learn to walk - they experiment and learn from the consequences.", "tokens_used": {"input": 1011, "output": 173, "cached": 0}}
